Relevant columns and their usage for this task  
• `event_date` : filter to the required calendar day (`'20210102'`).  
• `user_pseudo_id` : the anonymised user id – use it to isolate the single visitor (`'1402138.5184246691'`).  
• `event_name` : helps us keep only page‑view–type hits (normally `page_view`).  
• `event_params` : repeated record that actually contains the URL and title in the keys  
  – most frequently the keys are `page_location` (full URL), `page_path` (relative path), `page_title` (title).  
• `event_timestamp` : can be used for ordering the sequence of pages.  

Typical conditions  
‒ `event_date = '20210102'`  
‒ `user_pseudo_id = '1402138.5184246691'`  
‒ `event_name = 'page_view'` (optional when we only want page views)  

Below are ten progressively richer inspection queries (all limited to 20 rows) that lead up to the final answer.  
NB: only the table that contains 2‑Jan‑2021 data is referenced –  
`bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20210102`.

```sql
